The RFC adapter in SAP Cloud Platform Integration (CPI) facilitates communication with on-premise SAP systems,
specifically ECC systems, using Remote Function Call (RFC). It enables CPI to interact with function modules
within those systems, allowing for data exchange and process integration.

Key Features and Use Cases:

Integrating On-Premise Systems:
The RFC adapter enables seamless integration of on-premise ABAP systems (like ECC) with cloud-hosted
systems using the SAP Cloud Connector.

Function Module Execution:
It allows CPI to execute function modules within the ECC system, providing access to specific functionalities and data.

Request-Reply Communication:
The RFC adapter supports synchronous request-reply communication, enabling real-time interaction
between CPI and the on-premise system.

Cloud Connector Dependency:
For on-premise integration, the SAP Cloud Connector must be configured to establish a connection
between the cloud and the on-premise systems.

RFC Destination:
Before deploying an integration flow with the RFC adapter, an RFC destination must be created in
the SAP BTP cockpit, which specifies the connection details to the target ECC system.

XSD/WSDL for Function Modules:
You'll need the remote function module XSD (or WSDL) file, which defines the structure of
function module's input and output parameters, according to the SAP Help Portal.

Configuration Steps:
SAP Cloud Connector: Configure the SAP Cloud Connector to connect to the on-premise SAP system.
RFC Destination: Create an RFC destination in the SAP BTP cockpit, providing the necessary connection parameters.
Integration Flow:
Create an integration flow in CPI.
Use the Request-Reply step to initiate the RFC call.
Configure the RFC adapter in the receiver step.
Specify the RFC destination name.
Configure the function module name and parameters.
Deployment: Deploy the integration flow in CPI.


